---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Timeline from '../../components/Timeline.astro';
import { tracks, totalNotes, totalWeeks } from '../../data/roadmap';

const ready = tracks.flatMap((t) => t.notes).filter((n) => n.ready).length;
---

<Base title="Home">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">A {totalWeeks}-week path · {totalNotes} notes · 5 projects</p>
    <h1>AI Engineer Notes</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      From “what is a vector?” to designing LLM systems in an interview. Every idea is explained simply first, then
      precisely, then in words you can say out loud, and then practised until it sticks.
    </p>

    <p>
      These notes assume no AI background and no more math than school algebra. Each one starts with an everyday
      explanation, shows the idea as a picture, then gives the exact technical terms, the math and the code. It ends
      with a model interview answer, practice questions and a revision card.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with the <a href="/ai/roadmap/">roadmap</a> to see what to study each week, use the
      <a href="/ai/practice/">practice page</a> for the drills, and check the <a href="/ai/projects/">projects</a> you will
      build alongside. {ready} of {totalNotes} <a href="/ai/notes/">notes</a> are written so far. Reference pages sit
      beside the notes: the <a href="/ai/glossary/">glossary</a>, one plain sentence per technical word, and three
      code references, for <a href="/ai/numpy/">NumPy</a>, <a href="/ai/sklearn/">scikit-learn</a> and
      <a href="/ai/python/">Python tools</a>, one entry per function the code uses.
    </p>

    <h2>How these notes are written</h2>
    <p>
      Good explanations follow a few rules that teachers have known for a long time, and every note here is held to
      them. They are listed so you can hold the notes to them too: if a note breaks one, that is a bug.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing is used before it is taught.</strong> The notes are read in a fixed
        <a href="/ai/roadmap/">order</a>, and a note may only use words that an earlier note explained. When one must
        mention a later idea, the word is dotted-underlined and links to the glossary, with its one-line meaning on
        hover.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Concrete before abstract.</strong> Every idea starts with an everyday situation and small numbers
        (three houses, a coin, a recipe), then gets its picture, then its name, then its symbol. Experts tend to
        start with the symbol because it feels clear to them; it is not clear to a beginner, and we never do it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Plain words first in every section, not just the introduction.</strong> Each section opens with a
        “plain words” box that a bright fourteen-year-old could follow, and only then uses the exact terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ideas return in spirals.</strong> You meet the log loss as a forecaster’s score in week 1, as a
        formula in week 2, as maximum likelihood in week 3 and as cross-entropy in week 4. Each visit adds one layer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Worked examples, then your own.</strong> Every note works its numbers in full before asking you to,
        then gives about a dozen questions and sixteen problems, hand and code, with hidden solutions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Code uses only what has been explained.</strong> The code assumes plain Python and nothing else.
        Every library function and every less common bit of Python is introduced in a “New in this code” box right
        above the first block that uses it, in one plain sentence. Every name inside a code block is also a link to
        its entry on the <a href="/ai/numpy/">NumPy</a>, <a href="/ai/sklearn/">scikit-learn</a> or
        <a href="/ai/python/">Python tools</a> reference page. Code is written one step per line, and every output
        shown is real.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Only what an AI interview needs.</strong> Each topic earns its place by one of two tests: interviews
        ask it, or a later note needs it. The rest is left out on purpose and named in a “what you can skip” note.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <Timeline />

    <h2>The nine tracks</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Track</th><th>When</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            tracks.map((track) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <a href={`/ai/notes/#${track.id}`}>
                    {track.number}. {track.title}
                  </a>
                </td>
                <td>{track.weeks}</td>
                <td>{track.notes.length}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
